        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe new `OwnerCustomerCode` class is well-placed and idiomatic: it follows the established \"single source of truth\" helper pattern (like `OwnerIdentity`, `OwnerCheckDigit`, `OwnerTelephone`), reuses `OwnerTelephone.toE164` for normalization rather than duplicating it, and correctly owns both the construction (`customerCode`/`hash8`) and the read-back (`region`) of the code shape. `AssignOwnerCustomerCode` delegating to it and shedding its repository dependency is clean, and the downstream derivations (membership number, check digit, audit, locality) all read the stored code, so the \"derive everything from one code\" design holds together.\n\nBut the change gutted the old per-city sequence logic without cleaning up the structure that existed to support it, leaving stale cross-references:\n\n1. **`OwnerCity` is now orphaned as a shared primitive.** Its whole justification (javadoc at `OwnerCity.java:7-14`) is: *\"Both the city-capacity check (`CheckOwnerCityCapacity`) and the per-city customer-code sequence (`AssignOwnerCustomerCode`) depend on the same count ... so the definition ... lives here rather than being inlined in each.\"* After this change `AssignOwnerCustomerCode` no longer calls `countOwnersInCity` \u2014 `CheckOwnerCityCapacity` is the only remaining caller. The \"shared by two, extracted to stay consistent\" rationale is false, and a maintainer reading it would be misled. Either update the javadoc to reflect the single remaining caller, or inline `countOwnersInCity` back into `CheckOwnerCityCapacity` (the extraction no longer earns its keep with one caller).\n\n2. **`CheckOwnerCityCapacity` javadoc is stale** (`CheckOwnerCityCapacity.java:11-13`): *\"The city is matched exactly, consistent with `AssignOwnerCustomerCode`'s per-city sequence.\"* That per-city sequence no longer exists; the clause now points at nothing. Remove or rewrite it.\n\nNeither is in the production diff, but both are structural consequences the diff created and left incoherent. Fixing the new class's neighbors is part of landing this change cleanly."
      }
